  "summary": "A high exhaust gas temperature (EGT) indicates the turbine section is operating above its safe temperature, which can reduce component life and cause engine deterioration.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Excess EGT reduces turbine component life and can cause engine deterioration if operated above allowable temperatures.",
      "citations": [1]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": ""
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "The handbook defines a borescope as an inspection tool that allows visual examination of a turbine engine’s interior without disassembly.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "A borescope is an inspection tool consisting of a light, mirror, and magnifying lens mounted in a small‑diameter tube that is inserted through inspection ports to view the inside of a turbine engine without taking it apart.",
      "citations": [1]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": ""
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "The handbook states that a hot‑section inspection checks the combustion case and chambers for hot spots, leaks, distortion, overheating, cracks and wear, and examines turbine components (blades, guide vanes, disc, outlet ducts, nozzle) and fuel‑nozzle assemblies for cracks, warping, foreign‑object damage, stress‑rupture cracks, deformation and other damage.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The external combustion case is inspected for hot spots, exhaust leaks and distortions before it is opened.",
      "citations": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "After opening, combustion chambers are inspected for localized overheating, cracks and excessive wear.",
      "citations": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "First‑stage turbine blades and nozzle guide vanes are inspected for cracks, warping or foreign‑object damage (FOD).",
      "citations": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Combustion‑chamber outlet ducts and the turbine nozzle are inspected for cracks and evidence of FOD.",
      "citations": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Turbine discs are inspected for cracks, which require rejection if found.",
      "citations": [5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Turbine blades are inspected with strong light and magnification for stress‑rupture cracks and leading‑edge deformation.",
      "citations": [5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Fuel‑nozzle and support assemblies are cleaned and inspected for carbon deposits, nicks, burrs and damage.",
      "citations": [5]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": ""
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "The handbook defines the high‑pressure compressor (N2) as the second‑stage compressor in a dual‑spool gas‑turbine engine, forming the high‑pressure spool that supplies air to the combustion section and is rotated by the starter with its speed controlled by the fuel control.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The high‑pressure compressor, called the N2 compressor, is the second‑stage compressor in a dual‑spool engine, driven by the high‑pressure spool, rotated by the starter for starting, and its rpm is controlled by the fuel control.",
      "citations": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "In a dual‑spool engine the high‑pressure spool (which includes the N2 compressor) supplies air to the combustion section of the engine.",
      "citations": [1]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": ""
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "C‑MAPSS simulates engine degradation by applying exponential decay to the flow and efficiency parameters, starting from a small initial deterioration, generating a health‑index trajectory until it reaches zero, and adding process and measurement noise.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Degradation is modeled by varying the engine’s efficiency (e) and flow (f) parameters with an exponential rate of change, representing wear and fault progression.",
      "citations": [5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Initial deterioration values (e0, f0) are randomly selected within a 1 % deviation from the healthy condition, defining the start of each health‑index trajectory.",
      "citations": [6]
    },
    {
      "text": "A health index H(t) is computed from the evolving e(t) and f(t); the simulation stops when H reaches zero, which is defined as the failure criterion.",
      "citations": [6]
    },
    {
      "text": "Random process noise and measurement noise are superimposed on the simulated sensor outputs to emulate realistic data.",
      "citations": [6]
    },
    {
      "text": "The degradation simulations focus on the high‑pressure compressor (HPC) module under various altitude, thrust‑required‑power, and Mach‑number conditions, using sensed margins (fan, HPC, LPC, EGT) to compute the health index.",
      "citations": [4]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": ""
}

{
